    "created_at": "2025-11-19T19:07:13Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-11-19T19:11:01Z",
    "body": "Note: an I18N [best practice](https://www.w3.org/TR/international-specs/#application_internal) for enumerated keywords is to make them human legible, but obviously \"computerese\", so that developers will remember to wrap them in localizable display values and be less tempted to pipe them to the display (because they are already in English, which is only great if the rest of the display is in English too). All lowercase (`failure`) is fine. Shouted (`FAILURE`) is a bit better. Ugly (`RETURN_FAILURE`) is ideal, but, well, ugly and verbose. Values in Titlecase (`Failure`), such as in the original proposal, usually provoke me to write a comment like this because they look like already localized messages.\n\nAlso, if you go down this path, don't forget to define matching as _ASCII case-insensitive_ somewhere prominent (so that `failure` matches even when the runtime locale is e.g. Turkish).",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/infra/issues/comments/3554225647/reactions",
